.TH KILL 1
.SH NAME
kill \- terminate a process with extreme prejudice
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
.RB [ \- signo ]
processid ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Kill
sends signal 15 (terminate) to the specified processes.
If a signal number preceded by `\-' is given
as first argument, that signal is sent instead of
terminate
(see
.IR  signal (2)).
This will kill processes that do not catch the signal;
in particular `kill \-9 ...' is a sure kill.
.PP
By convention, if process number 0 is specified, all members
in the process group (i.e. processes resulting from 
the current login) are signaled.
.PP
The killed processes must belong
to the current user unless
he is the super-user.
To shut the system down and bring it up single user
the super-user may
use `kill \-1 1'; see
.IR init (8).
.PP
The process number of an asynchronous process
started with `&' is reported by the shell, and is also what the shell's
.B $!
expands to.
Process numbers can also be found by using
.IR ps (1).
.B Note:
.IR ps (1)
is not on this system yet, so
.B $!
and what the shell prints for a background job are the only ways to learn one.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`usage: ...' and exit status 2 if there is no process number, or if what stands
where one should is not a number.  Otherwise, one line per process that could not
be signalled, naming the number and the reason, and exit status 1.
.B Note:
v7 indexed
.I sys_errlist
for that reason with the subscript unchecked;
.IR strerror (3)
is used instead, which does the bound test.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ps(1), kill(2), signal(2), sh(1), perror(3)
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS `kill \-1 1' does not bring the system down here
The DESCRIPTION's recipe for going single-user assumes an
.IR init (8)
that catches SIGHUP and re-reads /etc/ttys.  There is one terminal on this
machine, no
.IR getty (8)
and no
.IR login (1),
so the shell you are typing at is the one
.I init
started and there is nothing to go back to.
.SS A signal is delivered on the way out of a system call
.I sysret()
runs
.IR issig ()/ psig ()
on every return to user mode, so a signal this program sends arrives as soon as
the target next leaves the kernel \- there is no separate delivery point and no
window in which a pending signal sits unnoticed.  See
.IR signal (2).
